# The machine looking at itself, and this collection looking at
# itself.  Some of these meet os9exec's limits rather than their
# own -- where they do, the screen says so.

shot    signal
cap     Sends an OS-9 signal to a process by id -- the id, the signal
cap     code, and seconds to wait first if you want a delay.  Code 0 ends
cap     the process.  Here `cron' is started in the background and bash
cap     prints the id it got, in angle brackets -- use the id you see
cap     rather than the 4 shown here.  Sent twice: the second answers 228,
cap     an illegal process id, which is how you know the first one landed.
try     signal 4 0
run     set +H
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     cron &
wait    4
run     signal 4 0; echo "status $?"
wait    4
run     signal 4 0; echo "status $?"
wait    4
shot    unc
size    34 80
cap     unc disassembling setime: the module header comes out as equates and a
cap     psect line, then the code, with each OS-9 system call named -- here
cap     I$WritLn writing the prompt and I$ReadLn reading the answer.  The
cap     first twenty-four lines; 2>/nil sets aside the form feeds it writes to
cap     the terminal as it works.
try     unc setime 2>/nil | head -n 24
os9     unc setime >>>/nil !head -n 24
run     builtin cd /dd/CMDS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     unc setime 2>/nil | head -n 24
wait    6

shot    about
size    30 100
cap     The collection's own guide: `about wump' answers what a program is,
cap     where it is filed and where it came from, whether its source survived,
cap     and how to run it and take it onto your own disk -- read out of the
cap     files the web catalogue is built from, at the moment you ask.
try     about wump
run     clear
wait    1
run     about wump
wait    5
shot    which
size    24 100
cap     Says what a command name runs, and where it came from.  It looks where
cap     the OS-9 shell looks -- memory, the execution directory, each PATH
cap     directory, then the data directory -- and stops at the first; -a lists
cap     every place.  A module already in memory is traced to the file that
cap     holds the same module, whatever the file is called: ckermit's module
cap     is named wermit.
try     which -a gnuchess
run     which cat
run     which -a gnuchess
run     load ckermit
run     which wermit
wait    6
shot    whereis
size    24 100
cap     Finds a program's source, its command and its documentation.  It
cap     searches SRC, CMDS, DEFS, LIB and DOC all the way down, on this disk and
cap     on every device PATH names, so source filed under the archive it came
cap     in -- gen's is in divutils -- still turns up.  Source is listed first,
cap     then commands, then documentation.
try     whereis gen
run     whereis gen
run     whereis keep tex
wait    6
shot    aprocs
cap     A process monitor: it prints the active processes as a tree -- id,
cap     parent, priority, CPU time, age and share of the CPU.  Here it catches
cap     the shell and itself.
try     aprocs
run     exec shell
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     aprocs
wait    5
shot    argproc_demo
cap     argproc_demo parsing a command line: argproc() takes the switches,
cap     fills in the defaults for the ones you left out, and hands back the
cap     filename.  A switch takes its argument with no space -- `-x99', never
cap     `-x 99'.
try     argproc_demo readme; argproc_demo -x99 -sHello readme; argproc_demo -help
run     argproc_demo readme
wait    6
run     argproc_demo -x99 -sHello readme
wait    6
run     argproc_demo -help
wait    8
shot    bigsetter
size    46 80
cap     bigsetter works through the BigSet library's operations on two sets
cap     of the numbers 0 to 35 -- include, exclude, union -- printing each
cap     set's members, or {} when it is empty, and waiting for a key after
cap     each; RETURN was pressed at every pause here.
try     bigsetter
run     clear
wait    1
run     bigsetter
wait    4
send    \r
wait    2
send    \r
wait    2
send    \r
wait    2
send    \r
wait    2
send    \r
wait    2
send    \r
wait    2
snap
kill
shot    bootgen
cap     bootgen builds the boot file a machine starts from -- the modules
cap     it loads before anything else, one after another in one file.
cap     Here it makes a new one, 256 sectors, on a RAM disk from two
cap     modules, wc and head; -h would also hook it into the disk's
cap     identification sector so the machine finds it, which is left alone
cap     here.  The RAM disk was made first with `mount -r=256k /r0'.
try     bootgen -n=256 /r0 wc head
run     mount -r=256k /r0 > /nil 2>/nil
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/CMDS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     bootgen -n=256 /r0 wc head
wait    6
run     ls -l /r0
wait    4
shot    clock
size    30 80
cap     A full-screen clock: the day, the date and the time in large
cap     letters, redrawn as the time changes.  It draws each line by running
cap     `banner' through `shell' -- your own OS-9's, which SYS/login loads
cap     from /h1 -- and reading the banner back from a pipe.  Control-C
cap     stops it.
try     clock
run     load /h1/CMDS/shell
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     clock
wait    10
snap
send    \003
wait    2
shot    config
cap     config reports this machine's C types as C comments: the size of
cap     char, short, int and long, their alignments and the pointer size,
cap     then the precision and range of float and double -- the first
cap     twenty lines.  It measures how much memory malloc can hand out by
cap     filling it, so the line beginning `#' is the trial emulator saying
cap     its memory is full: that is config's measurement, not a failure.
try     config | head -20
run     clear
wait    1
run     config | head -20
wait    5
shot    cpu
size    56 80
cap     cpu draws its heading and its speed chart and prints a first rate,
cap     0.06 MHz, then stops on a trap as its timing test starts, so the bar
cap     stays empty.  The register report below it is the trial emulator's
cap     account of the trap.
try     cpu
run     clear
wait    1
run     cpu
wait    10
shot    dam
cap     The disk allocation map, one character per 1K cluster: the first
cap     twenty lines of the volume the collection is on, every cluster in them
cap     in use.
try     dam /dd | head -n 21
os9     dam /dd !head -n 21
run     dam /dd | head -n 21
wait    10
shot    demo
size    30 80
cap     demo, the egetopt option-parsing example, showing how it read the
cap     command line it was given.
try     demo -a -b -c=7 one two
run     clear
wait    1
run     demo -a -b -c=7 one two
wait    6
shot    devprc
cap     devprc -a walks every process and lists the paths it holds open
cap     and the device behind each: here bash, the shell started from it,
cap     and devprc itself, all three on the terminal.
try     devprc -a
run     exec shell
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     devprc -a
wait    8
shot    ptxminst
cap     ptxminst installs Ptxm, the path-table kernel extension, and Ptxm
cap     must be resident first: `load CMDS/ptxm', then ptxminst.  Run
cap     without it, as here, it says nothing and returns 221, module not
cap     found.  With Ptxm loaded it installs it into system memory, which
cap     needs a real OS-9 kernel; os9exec stops it with a bus error.
try     ptxminst; echo "status $?"
os9     ptxminst
run     clear
wait    1
run     ptxminst; echo "status $?"
wait    6
shot    t_trtest
cap     t_trtest exercises a library's tree-search routines: each line typed
cap     is looked up, and one it does not find is added when you answer y.
cap     Here pear, apple and fig go in, then apple is found and deleted.  A
cap     line holding only a dot ends it.
try     t_trtest
run     clear
wait    1
run     t_trtest
wait    3
send    pear\r
wait    2
send    y\r
wait    2
send    apple\r
wait    2
send    y\r
wait    2
send    fig\r
wait    2
send    y\r
wait    2
send    apple\r
wait    2
send    y\r
wait    2
send    .\r
wait    3
shot    dinfo
cap     dinfo reports on a disk -- here the volume the collection is on: its
cap     name and when it was made, its capacity, and how much of it is free
cap     and in how many pieces.
try     dinfo /dd
run     dinfo /dd
wait    5
shot    dload
cap     Loads a data file into a data module in memory.  Given the message of
cap     the day it reports the size it read and the address the module was
cap     placed at.
try     dload motd
run     builtin cd /dd/SYS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     clear
run     dload motd
wait    7

shot    unkeep
size    30 80
cap     unkeep taking back what keep wrote for greg, which was kept beforehand
cap     into a scratch directory standing in for your disk: it reads the
cap     receipt in SYS/kept, removes each file it lists, and would leave any
cap     whose checksum had changed -- a score file, a saved game.
try     unkeep greg
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SYSB > /nil 2>/nil; export KEEP_TO=/dd/tmp/SYSB/kp; rm -rf /dd/tmp/SYSB/kp > /nil 2>/nil; keep greg > /nil
wait    5
run     clear
wait    1
run     unkeep greg
wait    5
shot    every
cap     every runs a command again and again at a fixed interval: `every 2
cap     today' asks today for the date and time in words every two seconds
cap     until you stop it -- the time moves on between answers.
try     every 2 today
run     clear
wait    1
run     every 2 today
wait    7
kill
shot    fastcc
cap     fastcc compiling DOC/samples/hello.c: -bp prints each command before
cap     it runs it -- cpp, c68, o68, r68 and l68 -- and -fd names the program
cap     it links in the current directory.  It drives your own OS-9's C
cap     compiler, so those five must be loaded or in your execution
cap     directory, with CDEF and CLIB naming your DEFS and LIB directories.
try     fastcc -bp -fd=hello hello.c; ls
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/G2cc > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/G2cc; cp /dd/DOC/samples/hello.c /dd/tmp/G2cc/hello.c
run     load /h1/CMDS/cpp /h1/CMDS/c68 /h1/CMDS/o68 /h1/CMDS/r68 /h1/CMDS/l68
run     export CDEF=/h1/DEFS CLIB=/h1/LIB
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/G2cc
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     fastcc -bp -fd=hello hello.c; ls
wait    25
shot    lfmaker
cap     lfmaker builds a G-Windows launch file.  Its first act is to ask
cap     the terminal for a G-Windows service; on a plain character terminal
cap     it is refused (E$UnkSvc) and it stops in silence with status 208,
cap     which is what the screen shows.  Run it in a G-Windows window.
try     lfmaker readme; echo "status $?"
os9     lfmaker readme
run     clear
wait    1
run     lfmaker readme; echo "status $?"
wait    6
shot    flink
cap     Shown by its usage alone.  Run on a disk, the entry it makes can leave
cap     the original file pointing at the wrong place, so it is not run here.
try     flink -?
run     flink -?
wait    5
shot    gen
size    30 80
cap     gen writes the frame of a new C program -- header block,
cap     authorship and version lines and sectioned comments.  It appends
cap     `.c' to the name you give, which is why it looks as though it
cap     wrote nothing: `gen -p demo' leaves demo.c, whose head is shown
cap     here.
try     gen -p tmp/SYSB/demo; cat tmp/SYSB/demo.c | head -n 16
os9     gen -p tmp/SYSB/demo; cat tmp/SYSB/demo.c !head -n 16
run     rm -f /dd/tmp/SYSB/demo.c > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SYSB > /nil 2>/nil
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     gen -p tmp/SYSB/demo
wait    5
run     cat tmp/SYSB/demo.c | head -n 16
wait    6
shot    getsys
size    24 80
cap     getsys reads OS-9's system globals field by field -- the module
cap     directory, the process descriptor table, the clock, the FPU the
cap     system believes it has -- about ninety lines, kept in a file here
cap     and shown from the top.  Under the trial emulator it was run
cap     beforehand, because the emulator prints a notice on the terminal
cap     for each global it does not keep; your own OS-9 prints none.
try     getsys > gs.out; head -n 20 gs.out
os9     getsys >gs.out; head -n 20 gs.out
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/G2gs > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/G2gs
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/G2gs
run     getsys > gs.out 2>/nil
wait    8
run     clear
wait    1
run     head -n 20 gs.out
wait    4
shot    hinterhalt
cap     Asked whether it needs to explain itself (J/N, German for Y/N) and
cap     told no, it draws the board: the walls, the player and a target.
try     hinterhalt
run     clear
wait    1
run     hinterhalt
wait    4
send    N\r
wait    6
snap
shot    testibc
cap     testibc's command menu, drawn once its timeout prompt is answered: bus
cap     commands to clear the interface, go remote or local and lock out the
cap     front panel, then send, receive and device clear.  Each of them talks
cap     to an IEEE-488 interface card, so the menu is as far as it goes
cap     without one.
try     testibc
run     clear
wait    1
run     testibc
wait    4
send    5\r
wait    4
snap
send    Q
wait    2
shot    i_am_i
size    62 80
cap     The Pascal quine printing its own source, from the header to the loop
cap     at the end that walks the table of lines.
try     i_am_i
run     clear
wait    1
run     i_am_i
wait    5
shot    liborder.os9
cap     Given relocatable objects, liborder.os9 reads the names each one uses
cap     and prints them in an order the linker can take in one pass, a module
cap     ahead of the ones it calls on: make.r first here, since it calls into
cap     the other two.  -modinfo lists an object's public and external names.
try     liborder.os9 make.r parse.r stat.r
run     builtin cd /dd/SRC/eff_make
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     liborder.os9 make.r parse.r stat.r
wait    5
run     liborder.os9 -modinfo stat.r
wait    5
shot    keep
size    30 80
cap     keep takes a program off the collection onto your own disk, copying
cap     the binary and the files DOC/DEPENDS says it reads, laid out as the
cap     program expects them.  Here greg goes to a scratch directory
cap     standing in for your disk on /h1; keep names each file it wrote and
cap     records it so unkeep can take it back.
try     keep greg
run     export KEEP_TO=/dd/tmp/SYSB/kp; rm -rf /dd/tmp/SYSB/kp > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SYSB > /nil 2>/nil
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     keep greg
wait    6
shot    kept
cap     kept lists what keep has written to your disk and what it came to.
cap     A program is kept first, then listed: each file, its size, and the
cap     total.
try     kept
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SYSB > /nil 2>/nil; export KEEP_TO=/dd/tmp/SYSB/kp; rm -rf /dd/tmp/SYSB/kp > /nil 2>/nil; keep greg > /nil
wait    5
run     clear
wait    1
run     kept
wait    5
shot    launch
cap     launch's usage: the shell to start, then the options -- -d to show
cap     what it reads and sets, -e for the editor bindings, -i to
cap     initialise the terminal.  What it sets comes from SYS/config, which
cap     you write: a line per port naming the terminal on it, such as
cap     /t1=vt100, and PATH= if you want one; PORT in the environment names
cap     the port it is starting on.
try     launch -?
run     clear
wait    1
run     launch -?
wait    6
shot    makecrc
cap     makecrc generates C source for six CRC polynomials, one file each,
cap     and says nothing -- the files it leaves are the output.  Run in a
cap     writable directory, then listed.
try     makecrc; ls
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/mc > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/mc > /nil 2>/nil
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/mc
run     clear
wait    1
run     makecrc
wait    4
run     ls
wait    3
shot    map
cap     The sectors DOC/INDEX occupies, as a starting sector and a count in
cap     hex -- here one segment -- and with -e the owner, attributes, dates,
cap     link count and size.
try     map -e DOC/INDEX
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     map -e DOC/INDEX
wait    5
shot    modinfo
size    28 80
cap     An OS-9 module header read back: name, size, owner, type and language,
cap     attributes, edition and header parity, then where the code starts and
cap     how much data and stack it asks for.  Every program on this disk is a
cap     module like this one.
try     modinfo CMDS/today
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     modinfo CMDS/today
wait    3
shot    names
term    dm1520
cap     names, a German address book, open: the fields of a record -- Anrede,
cap     Nachname, Vorname, Strasse, PLZ, Stadt, Telefon and two Bemerkung
cap     lines -- and below them the menu, a letter each: F finds, A adds a
cap     record, Q leaves.  It keeps its file under SYS and makes it on first
cap     run.  Its screen codes are built in: it places the cursor with
cap     control-^, column, row, as a Datamedia 1520 does, whatever TERM says.
try     names
run     clear
wait    1
run     names
wait    6
snap
send    Q
wait    2
kill
shot    ob68kdemo
size    34 80
cap     OmniBasic translating a BASIC program to C with -c: after its
cap     banner, the tail of the bench1.c it wrote from bench1.b, a timing
cap     loop from DOC/omnibasic, each BASIC line kept as a comment above
cap     the C it became.  Work in a copy of DOC/omnibasic, where its
cap     library and examples are.  Without -c it goes on to compile the C,
cap     which needs your own OS-9's cc.
try     ob68kdemo bench1.b -c; tail -l=16 bench1.c
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/G2ob > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/G2ob; cp /dd/DOC/omnibasic/bench1.b /dd/tmp/G2ob/bench1.b
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/G2ob
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     ob68kdemo bench1.b -c; tail -l=16 bench1.c
wait    12
shot    oskversion
cap     oskversion prints the OS-9 version the system reports -- the number,
cap     the CPU it thinks it is on, and the edition.
try     oskversion
run     clear
wait    1
run     oskversion
wait    3
shot    passwd
super
size    30 80
cap     passwd changing a password: it asks for the new one twice, echoing
cap     neither, and rewrites the user's line in SYS/password, which is
cap     plain text -- tester's line is shown before and after, the password
cap     now in its second field.  A user who already has a password is
cap     asked for it first.  It rewrites the file with your own OS-9's
cap     `copy', run through `shell', so both must be where it can find them.
try     passwd
run     load /h1/CMDS/shell /h1/CMDS/copy
run     ls /dd/tmp/G2pw.orig > /nil 2>/nil || cp /dd/SYS/password /dd/tmp/G2pw.orig
run     cp /dd/tmp/G2pw.orig /dd/SYS/password
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     grep tester SYS/password
wait    3
run     passwd
wait    4
send    lemon7\r
wait    3
send    lemon7\r
wait    6
run     grep tester SYS/password
wait    3
quit    cp /dd/tmp/G2pw.orig /dd/SYS/password\r
shot    phone
size    24 80
cap     phone draws its screen -- the title, EXIT, and your own terminal and
cap     user name -- then opens the terminal it was told to call.  There is no
cap     /t1 here, so it answers `Can't open other Terminal' and stops; on a
cap     system with a second terminal it rings the user there until answered,
cap     and control-E leaves.  With no path it prints its syntax.
try     phone /t1
run     phone /t1
wait    28
shot    scsiutil
size    32 80
cap     scsiutil's options, which it prints when asked with -?: inquiry,
cap     capacity, reading sectors, eject and load, and audio CD control --
cap     table of contents, play, volume -- with notes on each.  It talks to a
cap     SCSI device, and with none here the usage is what there is to show.
try     scsiutil -?
run     clear
wait    1
run     scsiutil -?
wait    8

shot    sddemo
super
cap     White's Speedisk 2.10, a disk defragmenter, drawing the map of a
cap     volume it is about to work on.
try     sddemo
run     sddemo
wait    10
shot    shdev
cap     shdev walks OS-9's device table and lists each device with its file
cap     manager, its driver and what it can do.  Here it prints the heading
cap     and no rows: the trial emulator keeps no device table for it to
cap     walk.  On your own OS-9 there is one row per device.  dinfo asks a
cap     named device directly, and does answer here.
try     shdev
run     clear
wait    1
run     shdev
wait    6
shot    spline
cap     A B-spline surface demonstration: it draws a fixed 5x5 mesh and the
cap     surface fitted through it in Tektronix graphics codes, which a
cap     character terminal shows as text.  It reads nothing -- the stream is
cap     the same whatever it is given -- and at the end waits for a key.
try     spline | head -n 14
os9     spline !head -n 14
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     spline | head -n 14
wait    5
shot    sqrtx
cap     The integers from 1 to 25 whose square root does not square back
cap     exactly: the root, its square, and how far off it lands.
try     sqrtx
run     sqrtx
wait    6
shot    ssl
super
cap     ssl shows a file's segment list: the sector its file descriptor
cap     sits in, then each run of sectors the file occupies, read off the
cap     raw disk.  It finds the file by name in the current directory, so
cap     change to the file's directory first.
try     ssl INDEX
run     builtin cd /dd/DOC
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     ssl INDEX
wait    4
shot    top
cap     top lists processes with their share of the CPU and refreshes every
cap     few seconds: plain `top' shows your own, busy or idle, and `top -a'
cap     everyone's.  The number is the interval; interrupt it to leave.
try     top 3
run     exec shell
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     top 3
wait    12
snap
kill

shot    UAC_view
size    24 80
cap     The session directory a UAC system monitor kept on a 68040 VMEbus
cap     machine in January 1996: when each session ran, how many processes
cap     it started and how many errors it logged.  The highlighted one opens
cap     its process pages; Q quits.  Run it in the UAC directory, with
cap     PORT naming your terminal.
try     PORT=/term UAC_view -w=DATA
os9     setenv PORT /term; UAC_view -w=DATA
run     cd UAC
run     clear
run     PORT=/term UAC_view -w=DATA
wait    6
snap
quit    Q

shot    dmode
fresh
size    28 80
cap     The parameters of the RAM disk's device descriptor, r0, loaded
cap     from your own OS-9 -- a disk's descriptor has to be resident for
cap     dmode to find it.  Hex fields are decoded: `ctl' here is no
cap     format, multi-sector I/O and autosize.  `key=n' after the device
cap     changes a field in memory.
try     dmode /r0
run     load /h1/CMDS/BOOTOBJS/r0
run     clear
run     dmode /r0
wait    3
shot    tty
cap     tty prints the name of the terminal it is run from -- here term, with
cap     no newline after it, so the prompt follows on the same line.
try     tty
run     clear
wait    1
run     tty
wait    5
shot    vecho
cap     vecho is System V's echo: it ends the line unless the text ends in
cap     \c.  The first line here stops at \c, so the second is written
cap     straight after it, on the same line.
try     vecho "one, \c"; vecho two
run     clear
wait    1
run     vecho "one, \c"; vecho two
wait    5
shot    ub68kdemo
size    34 80
cap     UniBasic 1.10 translating a BASIC program to C with -c: its banner,
cap     then the tail of the bench1.c it wrote from bench1.b, a timing loop
cap     from DOC/unibasic.  Work in a copy of DOC/unibasic, where its
cap     library and examples are.  Without -c it goes on to compile the C,
cap     which needs your own OS-9's cc.
try     ub68kdemo bench1.b -c; tail -l=16 bench1.c
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/G2ub > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/G2ub; cp /dd/DOC/unibasic/bench1.b /dd/tmp/G2ub/bench1.b
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/G2ub
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     ub68kdemo bench1.b -c; tail -l=16 bench1.c
wait    12
shot    ub68020demo
size    34 80
cap     The same UniBasic 1.10 built for the 68020, translating bench1.b to
cap     C: the line that differs from ub68kdemo is the processor its banner
cap     announces.
try     ub68020demo bench1.b -c; tail -l=16 bench1.c
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/G2u2 > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/G2u2; cp /dd/DOC/unibasic/bench1.b /dd/tmp/G2u2/bench1.b
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/G2u2
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     ub68020demo bench1.b -c; tail -l=16 bench1.c
wait    12
shot    vc
cap     vc, a spreadsheet: 42 entered in the cell at row 0, column 0, and
cap     below it a formula, r0c0*2, which shows its value, 84.  = enters a
cap     value or formula in the current cell, control-N and control-P move
cap     down and up, and `?' is its help.
try     vc
run     clear
wait    1
run     vc
wait    6
send    =42\r
wait    2
send    \016
wait    2
send    =r0c0*2\r
wait    3
snap
quit    \003
shot    version
cap     version prints its own version and nothing else, whatever module it
cap     is named: asked about today, it answers for itself.  modinfo shows a
cap     module's edition.
try     version CMDS/today
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     version CMDS/today
wait    4
shot    yagi
size    58 80
cap     yagi designs a Yagi antenna to DL6WU's method.  It asks for the
cap     frequency, the number of elements, the boom diameter, whether the
cap     elements are insulated from the boom, and a tubing size picked by
cap     its number from the list it prints; then it works out the lengths
cap     and spacings.  Here: 144 MHz, nine elements, a 20 mm boom, not
cap     insulated, tubing 4.
try     yagi
run     clear
wait    1
run     yagi
wait    4
send    144\r
wait    3
send    9\r
wait    4
send    20\r
wait    4
send    N\r
wait    5
send    4\r
wait    10
shot    cio
size    28 80
cap     Microware's C I/O trap module as `modinfo' reads it: a trap handler
cap     library (type $B), edition 6, which the starred programs call.  It is
cap     one of five Microware modules on this disk by permission -- cio, csl,
cap     csl020, math and math881 -- and SOURCES.txt records the grant.
try     modinfo CMDS/cio
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     modinfo CMDS/cio
wait    8
shot    csl
size    28 80
cap     Microware's C Shared Library, the trap module programs built with
cap     Ultra C call: a trap handler library (type $B), edition 25.  It ships
cap     here by Microware's permission, with cio, csl020, math and math881.
try     modinfo CMDS/csl
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     modinfo CMDS/csl
wait    8
shot    csl020
size    28 80
cap     The 68020/030/040 build of Microware's C Shared Library.  It registers
cap     under the same module name, csl, as a trap handler library, edition
cap     15.
try     modinfo CMDS/csl020
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     modinfo CMDS/csl020
wait    8
shot    math
size    28 80
cap     Microware's floating-point trap module, done in software: a trap
cap     handler library (type $B), edition 13.  It ships here by Microware's
cap     permission, with cio, csl, csl020 and math881.
try     modinfo CMDS/math
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     modinfo CMDS/math
wait    8
