shot    mtst
size    28 100
for     mtst
cap     mtst prints what each maths function returned beside what it was
cap     given: ceil, floor and round on the same number, stepping by a tenth,
cap     integer and floating side by side.  The first sixteen lines.
try     mtst | head -n 16
os9     mtst !head -n 16
run     clear
wait    1
run     mtst | head -n 16
wait    12
shot    fontgen
for     fontgen
cap     fontgen writes the source of a character font: an OS-9 data
cap     module in assembler, psect and all, ready for the 80-column card
cap     it was written for.  It reads its bitmap from standard input and
cap     will sit waiting on the terminal if you forget to give it one.
try     fontgen < /nil | head -n 10
os9     fontgen </nil !head -n 10
run     fontgen < /nil | head -n 10
wait    12
shot    dpark
cap     dpark restores an RBF disk's head to track 00 -- what you did before
cap     moving a drive.  It works in silence: here it parks the disk the
cap     collection is on and returns 0.
try     dpark /dd; echo "status $?"
os9     dpark /dd
run     clear
wait    1
run     dpark /dd; echo "status $?"
wait    5
shot    bsplt68
cap     A 9.5K boot file made by joining cat and clear with `cat', listed,
cap     then taken apart by bsplt68: it names each module as it writes it out,
cap     and the listing after shows cat and clear back as files of their own.
try     bsplt68 OS9Boot
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/BSPL > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/BSPL
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/BSPL
wait    1
run     cat /dd/CMDS/cat /dd/CMDS/clear > OS9Boot
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     ls -l
wait    3
run     bsplt68 OS9Boot
wait    6
run     ls -l
wait    4
shot    mexist
cap     mexist tests whether a module is in memory and sets its exit
cap     status -- zero when the module is there, one when it is not.
cap     bash is resident; nosuchmodule is not.
try     mexist bash; echo "status $?"; mexist nosuchmodule; echo "status $?"
run     mexist bash; echo "status $?"
wait    4
run     mexist nosuchmodule; echo "status $?"
wait    4
shot    time
size    24 80
cap     Times a program: it runs the command you give it, lets that command
cap     print whatever it prints, and then adds how long it took -- real
cap     time, and the processor time spent in the program and in the system.
try     time wc DOC/samples/jabber.txt
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     time wc DOC/samples/jabber.txt
wait    5

shot    vis
size    24 80
cap     vis runs a command over and over and redraws the screen with its
cap     output each time, the way `watch' does elsewhere.  Its header names
cap     the command, the delay between runs in seconds and the number of
cap     runs so far; here `today', once a second.  Control-C stops it.
try     vis -d1 today
run     clear
wait    1
run     vis -d1 today
wait    6
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    repeat
size    24 80
cap     repeat 2 today runs today twice, one after the other.  repeat hands
cap     the line to $SHELL, which SYS/login sets to ksh.
try     repeat 2 today
run     repeat 2 today
wait    4
shot    cron
size    25 80
cap     A scheduling daemon: it reads usr/lib/crontab -- shown first, and
cap     empty but for its comments -- and hands each command to Microware's
cap     shell at its minute, hour and day, running until it is stopped.
cap     Started in the background, bash prints the id it got in angle
cap     brackets, and `signal' sent to that id answers status 0: the daemon
cap     was there to take it.  Use the id you see.
try     cron &
run     set +H
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat usr/lib/crontab
wait    5
run     cron &
wait    4
run     signal 4 0; echo "status $?"
wait    4
shot    setime
super
cap     setime sets the system clock: it prompts for the date and time as
cap     one string, two digits each -- year, month, day, hour, minute,
cap     second, in that order.  Under os9exec the call that sets the time
cap     reports success and leaves the clock alone, so here it shows the
cap     prompt and the time as it was; on your own system the clock moves.
try     setime -?
run     setime -?
wait    5
shot    adduser
super
size    36 80
cap     adduser, run by the super-user, adding a login: it offers to pick
cap     the user number, writes the new line into SYS/password -- shown
cap     after -- and makes the user's home directory and a mail directory
cap     under $MAIL.  The password is left for the user to set with
cap     passwd.  Its usage lists -r to remove a user, which answers that
cap     removing is not implemented yet.
try     adduser alice
run     grep -v "^alice," /dd/SYS/password > /dd/tmp/G2pw.new; cp /dd/tmp/G2pw.new /dd/SYS/password
run     rm -rf /dd/USR/ALICE /dd/SPOOL/MAIL/ALICE /dd/LOG/alice.login > /nil 2>/nil
run     export MAIL=/dd/SPOOL/MAIL
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     adduser alice
wait    4
send    \r
wait    5
send    n\r
wait    5
run     grep alice SYS/password
wait    3
shot    combine
cap     combine interleaves two files byte by byte -- the even bytes from one,
cap     the odd bytes from the other -- the way a 16-bit EPROM image is
cap     rebuilt from its two 8-bit halves.  Eight A's and eight B's go in and
cap     nine AB pairs come out: it reports 16 bytes and writes one pair more
cap     than it read.  The file it makes has no permissions, so its owner
cap     gives it some with attr before reading it.
try     combine even odd mix; attr mix -r; cat mix; echo
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/G2cmb > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/G2cmb > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'AAAAAAAA' > /dd/tmp/G2cmb/even
run     printf 'BBBBBBBB' > /dd/tmp/G2cmb/odd
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/G2cmb
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     combine even odd mix
wait    3
run     attr mix -r; cat mix; echo
wait    3
shot    demerge
cap     demerge splits a file of several OS-9 modules back into one file
cap     per module.  OS-9's `merge' is concatenation, so `cat' makes the
cap     input: today and tty joined into one file, then taken apart into
cap     today and tty again.
try     demerge both; ls -l
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/dm > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/dm > /nil 2>/nil
run     cat /dd/CMDS/today /dd/CMDS/tty > /dd/tmp/UTILA/dm/both
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/dm
run     clear
wait    1
run     demerge both
wait    4
run     ls -l
wait    3
shot    ptob
size    46 80
cap     ptob turns a file of bit patterns back into characters, the other half
cap     of `btop': `Hello' becomes a row of eight marks per letter, a dot for
cap     each 0 bit and an O for each 1, and ptob reads those back to `Hello'.
try     cat bits; ptob bits > back; cat back; echo
os9     cat bits; ptob bits >back; cat back
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'Hello' > /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb/t
run     btop /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb/t > /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb/bits
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat bits
wait    2
run     ptob bits > back
wait    2
run     cat back; echo
wait    2
shot    trunc
cap     trunc cuts a file to a given length: `trunc <path> <num>'.  A
cap     sixteen-byte file is cut to eight, and the listing either side of
cap     it is the whole demonstration.
try     ls -l f; trunc f 8; ls -l f; cat f; echo
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/tr > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf '0123456789ABCDEF' > /dd/tmp/UTILA/tr/f
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/tr
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     ls -l f
wait    2
run     trunc f 8
wait    2
run     ls -l f; cat f; echo
wait    3
shot    clear
cap     clear clears the terminal screen.  A directory listing is printed
cap     and then wiped by clear -- only the line typed after it is left.
try     dir CMDS; clear; echo "the listing above was wiped by clear"
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     dir CMDS
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     echo "the listing above was wiped by clear"
wait    2
shot    lgrep
cap     lgrep lists the files a pattern appears in, names only: it runs
cap     `grep -l' and strips what grep adds, so the list can go straight
cap     onto another command line.  The grep it runs is your own OS-9's,
cap     loaded from /h1 -- this disk's GNU grep answers differently and
cap     lgrep prints nothing from it.  DOC/README-GREP compares the searchers.
try     lgrep ksh SYS/password SYS/login SYS/motd
run     load /h1/CMDS/grep
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     lgrep ksh SYS/password SYS/login SYS/motd
wait    4
shot    suse
cap     A CPU benchmark in 190 bytes of assembler: the Sieve of
cap     Eratosthenes run a thousand times, then it exits.  It prints
cap     nothing, so the time it takes is its result.
try     time suse
run     time suse
wait    8
shot    pri
cap     pri queues files for printing: it hands the names to `copy
cap     -w=/r0/list', run from /r0/cmds, so each lands in /r0/list for a
cap     spooler to take -- here the message of the day, listed after.  It
cap     needs a RAM disk at /r0 with your own OS-9's `copy' in /r0/cmds;
cap     for this card `mount -r=256k /r0' made one and copy was put there.
try     pri SYS/motd; ls -l /r0/list
run     mount -r=256k /r0
wait    2
run     mkdir -p /r0/cmds /r0/list
run     cp /h1/CMDS/copy /r0/cmds/copy
run     builtin cd /dd
run     clear
run     pri SYS/motd; ls -l /r0/list
wait    5

shot    snd_sig
size    26 80
cap     Sends a signal to one or more processes named by id.  With no
cap     option it sends the wake signal and says nothing; `-(n)' sends
cap     signal n, and 2 is the keyboard abort, which ends a process.  Here
cap     `cron' is started in the background and bash prints the id it got
cap     in angle brackets -- use the id you see: it is woken in silence, an
cap     id nothing holds is refused, and then cron is sent the abort.
try     snd_sig 4
run     set +H
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     cron &
wait    4
run     snd_sig 4
wait    3
run     snd_sig 99
wait    3
run     snd_sig -2 4; echo "status $?"
wait    4
shot    setime2
super
cap     setime2 sets the system date and time from the fields
cap     `yyy mm dd hh mm ss' at its prompt -- yyy counts years from 1900,
cap     so 126 is 2026 -- and prints the date.  os9exec does not move the
cap     clock, so what it prints is today's date, not the one typed.
try     setime2
run     clear
wait    1
run     setime2
wait    2
send    126 9 3 14 30 0\r
wait    3
shot    setyear
cap     setyear sets the year, and only the year, leaving the month, day
cap     and time alone: `setyear 2026'.  It prints the date it ends up
cap     with, which is what the card shows -- under os9exec the call that
cap     sets the time does nothing and reports success, so the date it
cap     prints is the one it started from.  On your own system the year
cap     changes.
try     setyear 2026
run     setyear 2026
wait    3
shot    suspend
cap     suspend's usage, which names what it does: it removes a process
cap     from the system rather than pausing it, and -s reaches system
cap     programs too.  It is for the super-user: run by anyone else it
cap     answers that only system super-users may use it.
try     suspend -?
run     clear
wait    1
run     suspend -?
wait    4
shot    umusek
cap     umusek is a music editor.  It wants a hardware graphics screen and
cap     stops without one: `***DS_ScAdd Error 208.', then `Fran: Can't get
cap     screen addr, 'bye!', and status 37.
try     umusek; echo "status $?"
run     umusek; echo "status $?"
wait    5
shot    unpacklib.os9
cap     unpacklib.os9 extracts the object modules from an OS-9 library into
cap     separate .r files.  Run on a copy of alib.l, a library of C functions,
cap     it writes one file per module into the current directory, listed
cap     afterwards.
try     unpacklib.os9 alib.l; ls
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/ul > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/ul > /nil 2>/nil
run     cp /dd/LIB/alib.l /dd/tmp/UTILA/ul/alib.l
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/ul
run     clear
wait    1
run     unpacklib.os9 alib.l
wait    5
run     ls
wait    3
shot    ynad
term    dm1520
size    24 80
cap     YNAD, Yet Another Name & Address program, opened: its form -- code,
cap     surname, first name, two address lines, city, state, zip code and
cap     phone -- the menu of actions below it, and its first question, the
cap     name of the file to use.  Its screen codes are built in, a Datamedia
cap     1520's, whatever TERM says.
try     ynad
run     ynad
wait    8
snap
send    q
wait    2
shot    bootlogger
super
cap     bootlogger says nothing and returns 0; the line it wrote to
cap     SYS/bootlog is the proof it ran.  Run by hand it stamps the time you
cap     ran it.  It prints the year as 19 followed by OS-9's years since
cap     1900, so 2026 comes out as 19126.
try     bootlogger; echo "status $?"; cat SYS/bootlog
os9     bootlogger; cat SYS/bootlog
run     rm -f /dd/SYS/bootlog > /nil 2>/nil
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     bootlogger; echo "status $?"
wait    4
run     cat SYS/bootlog
wait    3
shot    screen_nocio
cap     screen_nocio is the trap-free build of `screen', a login greeting:
cap     it picks a file at random from $HOME/.screens and runs it through
cap     `shell' -- your own OS-9's, which SYS/login loads from /h1.  Here
cap     the one file holds `today', so the greeting is the date in words.
try     screen_nocio
run     load /h1/CMDS/shell
wait    3
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/home > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/home/.screens > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'today\r' > /dd/tmp/UTILA/home/.screens/hi
run     export HOME=/dd/tmp/UTILA/home
run     clear
wait    1
run     screen_nocio
wait    6

shot    transfer
cap     transfer copies files from GDOS disks to OS-9 and takes no options.
cap     It wants the GDOS device DGDOS0, which is not present, and says so.
try     transfer
run     transfer
wait    4
send    \r
wait    2
shot    preset
cap     What preset writes to the terminal, caught in a file and shown by
cap     dump: each key's definition is $1C, the key's code from $80 up, and
cap     the text the key will send -- dir, umacs, r68, l68, dsave -ieb128k
cap     and so on -- then `Funktionstasten belegt!', keys assigned.
try     preset > keys; dump keys
os9     preset >keys; dump keys
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/G2ps > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/G2ps
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/G2ps
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     preset > keys; dump keys
wait    6
shot    vlen
size    24 80
cap     vlen is a variable-length-record demonstration: it builds a small
cap     filesystem of its own, writes a hundred records of differing
cap     length, and reads them back deleting some as it goes.  The tail of
cap     the run, ending `vlTest completed successfully'.
try     vlen > vl.out; tail -l=16 vl.out
os9     vlen >vl.out; tail -l=16 vl.out
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/vl > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/vl > /nil 2>/nil
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/vl
run     clear
wait    1
run     vlen > vl.out
wait    30
run     tail -l=16 vl.out
wait    4
shot    mvolformat
size    80 80
cap     mvolformat formats a set of disks with numbered volume names: here
cap     two passes over a RAM disk, named "Data Vol 1" and "Data Vol 2".
cap     Before each it waits for RETURN, the moment to change the floppy,
cap     then runs your own OS-9's `format', which must be resident.  `free'
cap     afterwards reads the name the last one wrote.
try     mvolformat /r0 Data 2
run     mount -r=256k /r0 > /nil 2>/nil
run     load /h1/CMDS/format
run     load /h1/CMDS/free
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     mvolformat /r0 Data 2
wait    4
send    \r
wait    8
send    \r
wait    8
run     free /r0
wait    3
shot    rndir
size    24 80
cap     Two subdirectories, Notes and Papers, turned to lower case by rndir -l
cap     and listed afterwards.  It needs your own OS-9's dir, rename and del
cap     loaded -- this disk's dir answers differently, and rndir then does
cap     nothing.
try     rndir -l
run     load /h1/CMDS/dir
run     load /h1/CMDS/del
run     load /h1/CMDS/rename
run     load /h1/CMDS/shell
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/RNDIRC; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/RNDIRC/Notes /dd/tmp/RNDIRC/Papers
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/RNDIRC
run     clear
run     rndir -l
wait    6
run     ls
wait    2

shot    freeb
size    30 80
cap     freeb lists a disk's free space block by block.  Bare, it prints
cap     the volume name and when it was made, the size of the disk, and
cap     each free block with where it starts and how many sectors it holds;
cap     -t counts the blocks by size instead.  -a lists every one and -h
cap     leaves the header out.
try     freeb; freeb -t
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     freeb
wait    3
run     freeb -t
wait    4

shot    os9dsk
size    30 80
cap     Reads a Color Computer .DSK disk image -- the format a CoCo emulator
cap     keeps a floppy in -- and lists the OS-9 disk inside it: the root
cap     first, then its CMDS directory.  The image here is a public-domain
cap     disk from 1985.  -get copies a file out of it; -proc prints a
cap     procedure file of makdir, chd and -get lines that extracts the whole
cap     tree.
try     os9dsk -dir PUBDOM19.DSK; os9dsk -dir PUBDOM19.DSK CMDS
run     builtin cd /dd/DOC/os9dsk
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     os9dsk -dir PUBDOM19.DSK
wait    4
run     os9dsk -dir PUBDOM19.DSK CMDS
wait    5

shot    rsdsk
size    34 80
cap     Reads the RS-DOS side of a CoCo .DSK image -- Disk Extended BASIC
cap     disks, where a name is eight characters and three, and a file is
cap     BASIC, binary, data or text.  The image here holds a menu program, a
cap     music player and its tunes.
try     rsdsk -dir PUBDOM09.DSK
run     builtin cd /dd/DOC/rsdsk
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     rsdsk -dir PUBDOM09.DSK
wait    5

shot    zc
size    24 80
cap     A US postal zip code in, the city and state out.  The table it reads
cap     is three quarters of a megabyte of them, one line per code, and a
cap     code that is not in the table is told so rather than guessed at.
cap     With no argument it opens a small form instead, where C clears the
cap     field and Q leaves.
try     zc 50325
run     clear
wait    1
run     zc 50325
wait    3
run     zc 02139
wait    3
run     zc 99999
wait    3

shot    resize
size    30 100
cap     Asks the terminal how big its window is and prints LINES and COLUMNS,
cap     for less and the other programs that read them before the termcap's
cap     24 by 80.  This window is 30 by 100.  At the bash prompt SYS/login
cap     gives you, typing `resize' sets the two; -s prints them for your own
cap     OS-9's shell instead.  A terminal that does not answer gets a second,
cap     and then resize prints nothing.
try     resize
run     clear
wait    1
run     resize
wait    4
run     resize -s
wait    4
